var o={kind:"fibroscan"},r={kind:"plane",plane:"csph"},s=365,e="Hepatitis C cured",t="A higher start",a="MASLD",i="Alcohol stopped",n={id:"cause-treated",level:"core",title:"Treating the cause",minutes:10,objectives:["Explain why curing hepatitis C, suppressing hepatitis B, alcohol abstinence or weight loss lowers portal pressure over months to years","Use liver stiffness measurement (LSM) to judge whether CSPH has resolved after cure","Recognize that CSPH can persist after cure when the HVPG starts high","Interpret LSM in MASLD with obesity, and describe the effect of weight loss on it"],checks:[["After hepatitis C is cured, which LSM shows that CSPH has resolved?","LSM <10 kPa, or LSM <15 kPa with a spleen stiffness (SSM) <25 kPa (Baveno VIII)."],["Does a cure always remove CSPH?","No: when the HVPG starts high, CSPH often persists, and the beta-blocker continues."],["Why does the 25 kPa rule-in not apply at a BMI \u226530?","A high LSM often overestimates portal pressure in obesity, so ANTICIPATE-NASH, which adds the BMI, is used instead."],["Two years after hepatitis C cure, his LSM has fallen from 15 to 9 kPa and his platelets have risen to 180,000/\xB5L. Has CSPH resolved, and what continues?","Yes: by Baveno VIII, an LSM <10 kPa after cure means CSPH has resolved. HCC surveillance every 6 months continues in anyone who had cirrhosis."]],sections:[[e,[e]],[t,[t]],[a,[a]],[i,[i]]],summary:"Four patients whose cause is treated: hepatitis C cured early, hepatitis C cured with CSPH that persists, MASH cirrhosis with weight loss, and alcohol-associated cirrhosis after abstinence. LSM shows whether CSPH has resolved.",slides:[{id:"before",preset:"csph",cam:"liver",tool:o,data:"tiles",tiles:["lsm","plt","hvpg"],key:["hvpg"],kicker:e,site:"sin",title:"Hepatitis C cirrhosis with CSPH, before treatment",vignette:"A 52-year-old man with hepatitis C cirrhosis, diagnosed at blood donation, starts sofosbuvir and velpatasvir. He feels well and has never had ascites or bleeding.",line:"A liver stiffness (LSM) of {lsm} with platelets of {plt} places him in the indeterminate zone; an HVPG of {hvpg} confirms CSPH, so carvedilol starts with the antivirals.",notes:"Curing hepatitis C does not lower the portal pressure at once, so a patient with CSPH starts carvedilol with the antivirals. In the indeterminate zone between 15 and 25 kPa, Baveno VIII calls CSPH present when a validated model such as ANTICIPATE puts its probability at 75% or more. Here the HVPG decides. Note: in the model the patient is off carvedilol, so the changes that follow come from treating the cause alone.",ask:["Why start carvedilol now, if the virus will soon be cured?","The portal pressure falls only slowly after cure, and carvedilol lowers the risk of decompensation in the meantime."]},{id:"year1",cutouts:["lobule"],lens:"since",days:365,ramp:{cirrhosis:[.6,.45]},lapse:{seconds:8,from:"Cure",to:"One year later"},cam:"liver",tool:{kind:"flowtime",clock:"days",lines:["hvpg","varix"],title:"HVPG and varix size over the year"},data:"tiles",tiles:["hvpg","varix","lsm","plt"],key:["lsm"],delta:"before",kicker:e,site:"sin",title:"A year after cure, the HVPG and the LSM fall",line:"The LSM is {lsm}, the platelet count has risen to {plt} and the HVPG has fallen to {hvpg}.",notes:"After cure, hepatic inflammation resolves first, so the LSM falls faster than fibrosis regresses, and an early fall overstates fibrosis regression. The HVPG falls more slowly and less completely, and in some patients with CSPH before treatment it stays \u226510 mmHg. Whether carvedilol can be stopped once CSPH resolves is not established.",ask:["The LSM falls quickly in the first year after cure. Has the fibrosis regressed as quickly?","No: much of the early fall reflects resolving inflammation, so the LSM overstates the regression."]},{id:"year2",cutouts:["lobule","sinusoid"],days:365,ramp:{cirrhosis:[.45,.32]},lapse:{seconds:8,from:"One year",to:"Two years after cure"},cam:"liver",tool:{kind:"flowtime",clock:"days",lines:["hvpg","ssm"],keepTiles:!0,title:"HVPG and spleen stiffness over the year"},data:"tiles",tiles:["hvpg","ssm"],key:["hvpg"],delta:"before",kicker:e,site:"sin",title:"Two years after cure, CSPH has resolved",line:"The LSM is {lsm}, which is {<10 kPa}, and the platelet count {plt}: by Baveno VIII, CSPH has resolved.",notes:"After treating the cause, Baveno VIII advises reassessing CSPH, and counts it as resolved at an HVPG <10 mmHg, an LSM <10 kPa, or an LSM <15 kPa with a spleen stiffness (SSM) <25 kPa; his SSM tile, the model's value, has fallen below 25 kPa with the lower pressure and the smaller spleen. HCC surveillance every 6 months continues, because cirrhosis was present before the cure. Repeat the LSM and platelet count if a new cause of liver injury appears.",ask:["Does he still need HCC surveillance?","Yes, every 6 months: cirrhosis was present before the cure, and the HCC risk remains."]},{id:"year2-plane",cam:"liver",tool:{...r,trail:[{slide:"before",name:"Before cure"},{slide:"year1",name:"1 year"}]},data:"tiles",tiles:["hvpg","ssm"],key:["hvpg"],delta:"before",kicker:e,site:"sin",title:"On the CSPH plane, the trail ends where CSPH has resolved",line:"The trail runs from before the cure, through one year, to two years after it.",notes:"The same man as the slide before, two years after the cure. The plane marks where he stood before the cure, at one year and now, against the CSPH criteria.",ask:["What does the trail across the plane show?","The HVPG and LSM fall after the cure, until CSPH has resolved."]},{id:"path-stopcarv",cam:"liver",tool:{kind:"path",of:"stopcarv",short:"Stopping carvedilol",patient:{hv:"none"}},kicker:e,site:"sin",title:"With CSPH resolved, carvedilol may be stopped",line:"His LSM is {lsm}, which is {<10 kPa}: CSPH is ruled out without HVPG measurement, so the algorithm allows carvedilol to be stopped. Surveillance for hepatocellular carcinoma continues.",notes:"Source: Baveno VIII 3.14, 7.15 to 7.19. After etiologic treatment CSPH can persist and should be reassessed (3.14b). Carvedilol may be stopped only if the resolution is confirmed (7.18, weak): by an HVPG under 10 mmHg measured off carvedilol (7.16), or by LSM, which his chart uses: an LSM <10 kPa, or <15 kPa with an SSM <25 kPa (7.19). An LSM of 9.1 kPa meets the first. Varices and collaterals may persist after CSPH has resolved, so they are no reason to keep the drug (7.17). Statements 7.15 to 7.19 are written for recompensated patients; he was never decompensated, so the chart applies by extension, as the guideline's 3.14b asks for CSPH to be reassessed after cure. His LSM falls faster than fibrosis regresses, so many centers repeat it, or measure an HVPG off carvedilol, before stopping. The model shows him off carvedilol throughout.",ask:["His LSM is 12 kPa and his SSM 30 kPa. Can carvedilol be stopped?","No: 12 kPa is not <10, and <15 kPa counts only with an SSM <25 kPa. Measure the HVPG off carvedilol, or repeat the LSM later."]},{id:"high",preset:"csph",params:{cirrhosis:.75},days:365,cam:"liver",tool:{kind:"profile"},data:"tiles",key:["hvpg"],tiles:["varix","asc"],kicker:t,site:"sin",title:"A higher HVPG before cure",vignette:"A 58-year-old woman with hepatitis C cirrhosis from a transfusion in 1987 starts sofosbuvir-velpatasvir. EGD shows large varices, and ultrasound shows grade 1 ascites.",caseQ:"Will cure remove her CSPH?",line:"With an HVPG of {hvpg}, large varices and ascites seen only on ultrasound, she starts carvedilol with the antivirals.",notes:"Ascites seen only on ultrasound does not count as decompensation, so she is still compensated, with CSPH and high-risk varices. Carvedilol treats both: it lowers the risk of a first variceal bleed and of decompensation. The higher the HVPG before cure, the more likely CSPH is to persist after it. In the model she is off carvedilol, so the changes that follow come from treating the cause alone.",ask:["Ascites seen only on ultrasound: is she decompensated?","No: only clinically evident ascites marks decompensation, though even ascites seen only on ultrasound carries a worse prognosis."]},{id:"high2",days:730,ramp:{cirrhosis:[.75,.57]},lapse:{seconds:10,from:"Cure",to:"Two years later"},cam:"liver",tool:{kind:"flowtime",clock:"days",lines:["hvpg","ssm"],keepTiles:!0,title:"HVPG and spleen stiffness over two years"},data:"tiles",key:["hvpg"],tiles:["hvpg","ssm"],delta:"high",kicker:t,site:"sin",title:"Two years after cure, CSPH persists",line:"Her LSM of {lsm} is in the indeterminate zone and cannot decide alone. The HVPG is {hvpg}, which is {>=10 mmHg}: CSPH persists and she continues carvedilol.",notes:"When the HVPG starts well above 10 mmHg, CSPH often persists after cure, and so does the risk of decompensation. She continues carvedilol and HCC surveillance every 6 months. Her LSM, still in the indeterminate zone, cannot show on its own whether CSPH has resolved.",ask:["Should she stop carvedilol now that the virus is gone?","No: CSPH persists, so carvedilol continues."]},{id:"high2-plane",cam:"liver",tool:{...r,trail:[{slide:"high",name:"Before cure"}]},data:"tiles",tiles:["hvpg","ssm"],key:["hvpg"],delta:"high",kicker:t,site:"sin",title:"On the CSPH plane, the trail stays inside the CSPH region",line:"The trail runs from before the cure to two years later and does not leave the CSPH region, so carvedilol continues.",notes:"The same woman as the slide before, two years after the cure. The plane marks where she stood before the cure and now, against the CSPH criteria.",ask:["What does the trail across the plane show for her?","The HVPG falls after the cure but stays in the CSPH region, so CSPH persists."]},{id:"masld",preset:"cirr-comp",params:{cirrhosis:.45},relax:s,days:365,ramp:{cirrhosis:[.45,.35]},lapse:{seconds:8,from:"Weight loss starts",to:"One year later"},cam:"liver",tool:{kind:"flowtime",clock:"days",lines:["wt","lsm"],given:{wt:12},title:"Body weight and LSM over the year"},data:"tiles",tiles:["lsm","plt"],key:["lsm"],kicker:a,site:"sin",title:"A 12% weight loss lowers her LSM",vignette:"A 54-year-old woman with MASH cirrhosis and a BMI of 34 has an LSM of 15 kPa by VCTE. Her platelets are 138,000/\xB5L, and she has never had ascites or bleeding.",line:"A 12% weight loss over a year lowers her LSM from the indeterminate zone to {lsm} and raises her platelets to {plt}, which now rules CSPH out.",notes:"At the start her LSM is in the indeterminate zone, and at a BMI \u226530 an LSM \u226525 kPa no longer rules CSPH in, because it often overestimates portal pressure in obesity. Baveno VIII then uses ANTICIPATE-NASH, which adds the BMI, and calls CSPH present at a probability of 75% or more. In MASLD-related cACLD, Baveno VIII (2.13) links a weight loss of \u226510% with a fall in LSM of \u226530%. A confirmed change of \u226530% goes with a meaningful change in the risk of liver-related events, particularly when the LSM crosses 15 kPa (2.9). Diet and exercise come first; GLP-1-based therapies can be used for their approved indications, obesity included, and bariatric surgery suits selected patients without CSPH, and patients with CSPH only at expert centers. An LSM \u226415 kPa with platelets \u2265150,000/\xB5L rules CSPH out, whatever the etiology. Note: the model has no steatosis or body weight. On the chart, body weight falls from 100% to 88%, the 12% named on the slide, spread evenly over the year with fibrosis regressing in step, and the LSM line comes from the model.",ask:["How much weight loss changes the LSM meaningfully?","\u226510% of body weight, which goes with a fall in LSM of \u226530%."]},{id:"alcohol",preset:"csph",params:{cirrhosis:.7},relax:s,days:365,ramp:{cirrhosis:[.7,.55]},lapse:{seconds:8,from:"Last drink",to:"One year abstinent"},cam:"liver",tool:{kind:"flowtime",clock:"days",lines:["hvpg","lsm"],keepTiles:!0,title:"HVPG and LSM over the year"},data:"tiles",tiles:["hvpg","lsm"],key:["hvpg"],kicker:i,site:"sin",title:"A year of abstinence lowers the HVPG",vignette:"A 49-year-old man with alcohol-associated cirrhosis and CSPH, Child\u2013Pugh A 6, stops drinking after his first clinic visit. He has medium esophageal varices and no ascites.",line:"After a year of abstinence, his HVPG has fallen to {hvpg} and his LSM to {lsm}. A relapse to drinking would raise the HVPG again.",notes:"Abstinence is the etiologic treatment in alcohol-associated cirrhosis, and the HVPG falls within months of the last drink, more in those who stop completely than in those who cut down; continued drinking raises it. Much of the early fall in LSM reflects resolving inflammation, so the LSM is best interpreted after some months of abstinence. Support to maintain abstinence is part of the treatment; the choice of counseling and pharmacotherapy rests with an addiction specialist, because neither guideline cited here names a drug. Carvedilol continues while CSPH persists, and HCC surveillance every 6 months continues for life. In the model fibrosis regresses evenly over the year, with no relapses.",ask:["How soon after stopping alcohol does the portal pressure fall?","Within months, more with complete abstinence; a relapse raises it."]},{id:"allpatients",cam:"fit",visual:"allpatients",cohort:[{name:"Hepatitis C",c:"s1",slides:["before","year1","year2"]},{name:"Higher start",c:"s2",slides:["high","high2"]},{name:"MASLD",c:"s3",slides:["masld"],pace:!0,clear:"Ruled out"},{name:"Alcohol",c:"s7",slides:["alcohol"],pace:!0}],kicker:"Summary",title:"Where each patient started decides where they end",line:"Two years on, only the patient who started at {hvpg@high} is still {>=10 mmHg}; the other three are {<10 mmHg}.",chartFoot:["* Dotted: a second year the deck has no slide for, the model with year one\u2019s pace kept.","LSM is liver stiffness in kPa, Plt platelets in \xD710\xB3/\xB5L. All four are off carvedilol."],notes:"The four patients on one chart, the HVPG over two years with the CSPH line at 10 mmHg. The 1 and 2 year values stand on the lines, and the key gives the HVPG, the LSM and the platelet count before treatment, at 1 year and at 2 years, the patient with the highest pressure first. The hepatitis C patients are the deck\u2019s own slides. The MASLD and alcohol patients have one year in the deck; their second year is the model with fibrosis regressing at the same pace as in the first, which takes the alcohol patient under 10 mmHg too. Patients vary far more than the model does, and the pressure can stop falling. The one who starts highest, at 14.7 mmHg, is still above the line after two years, so she continues carvedilol.",ask:["Which of the four still has CSPH at two years, and why?","The woman who started at 14.7 mmHg: cure lowered her HVPG only to 10.8 mmHg, which is \u226510. The others fell to <10."]},{id:"relation",preset:"csph",params:{cirrhosis:.75},days:730,ramp:{cirrhosis:[.75,.57]},cam:"liver",tool:{kind:"curve",x:"lsm",xCuts:[10],cuts:[10],patient:{preset:"healthy"},param:"cirrhosis",from:.3,to:.85,step:.05,days:365,lines:["hvpg","plt"],dots:[{slide:"before",name:"First patient",on:[0],walk:"first"},{slide:"year1",on:[0],walk:"first"},{slide:"year2",name:"CSPH gone",on:[0],walk:"first"},{slide:"high",name:"Higher start",on:[0],walk:"second"},{slide:"high2",name:"CSPH persists",on:[0],walk:"second"}]},kicker:"Summary",site:"sin",title:"Where the HVPG starts decides whether CSPH resolves",line:"Cure took the first patient from {hvpg@before} to {hvpg@year2}, which is {<10 mmHg}. The second starts at {hvpg@high} and is at {hvpg} two years on, still {>=10 mmHg}.",notes:"LSM on the x-axis, the HVPG and the platelet count on the y-axis: the model's cirrhosis from mild to severe, each point at steady state after a year. Curing the cause moves a patient down this line, not off it. Both patients are cured of hepatitis C, but the second starts higher, at an HVPG of 14.7 mmHg against 11.6, and in the model her fibrosis regresses less over the two years, so she ends at 10.8 mmHg while he ends at 4.8 mmHg, with an LSM <10 kPa. Baveno VIII counts CSPH as resolved at an HVPG <10 mmHg, an LSM <10 kPa, or an LSM <15 kPa with an SSM <25 kPa (Fig. 2): the dashed lines mark 10 on both axes. Removing the cause lowers the HVPG in most patients, and how far it must fall to prevent decompensation is not yet established (Table 3). One model patient per point; real recoveries vary far more. The MASLD patient is left off: at a BMI \u226530 the LSM overestimates portal pressure, so the line does not describe her (1.16). (Baveno VIII Fig. 2, Table 3, 1.16.)",ask:["Both patients were cured of hepatitis C. Why has CSPH resolved in the man but not in the woman?","She started higher, at an HVPG of 14.7 mmHg, and the pressure falls only part of the way in two years, so she stays \u226510 mmHg; he started at 11.6 mmHg and fell to <10 mmHg."]},{id:"summary",visual:"table",cols:["hvpg","lsm","plt"],asc:!1,rowHead:"Patient",of:[{id:"before",kicker:"Hepatitis C",title:"Before cure"},{id:"year1",kicker:"Hepatitis C",title:"One year after cure",vs:"before"},{id:"year2",kicker:"Hepatitis C",title:"Two years after cure",vs:"before"},{id:"high",kicker:"Higher start",title:"Before cure",ref:!0},{id:"high2",kicker:"Higher start",title:"Two years after cure",vs:"high"},{preset:"cirr-comp",params:{cirrhosis:.45},relax:s,key:"masld0",kicker:"MASLD",title:"Before weight loss",ref:!0},{id:"masld",kicker:"MASLD",title:"After 12% weight loss",vs:"masld0"},{preset:"csph",params:{cirrhosis:.7},relax:s,key:"alc0",kicker:"Alcohol",title:"Still drinking",ref:!0},{id:"alcohol",kicker:"Alcohol",title:"A year abstinent",vs:"alc0"}],kicker:"Summary",title:"How far the pressure falls depends on where it started",line:"Treating the cause lowers the HVPG in most patients, but CSPH persists when it starts high.",notes:"Curing hepatitis C, suppressing hepatitis B, stopping alcohol or losing weight is the most effective long-term treatment of portal hypertension, but the pressure falls over years, not weeks. Patients who start with a high HVPG often keep CSPH and keep their beta-blocker. HCC surveillance continues after any cure in a patient who had cirrhosis. Hepatitis B behaves like hepatitis C here: entecavir or tenofovir, taken long term, suppresses the virus, and the LSM and the HVPG fall over years; the HCC risk falls but does not disappear.",ask:["After cure, who still needs carvedilol?","Anyone in whom CSPH persists, as shown by the HVPG or by LSM and platelet count."]}]};export{n as a};
